Introduction
Accelerated MRI acquires sub-sampled k-space in order to reduce scanning time. Such acquisition leads to image-domain aliasing, which is equivalent to performing a convolution using a kernel that covers the entire field of view (FOV), with the underlying aliasing-free image (Fig. \ref{fig:acceleratedMRI}a). Reconstruction is essentially to reverse such a convolution process. Due to hardware limitations, k-space data along read-out (RO) direction is always fully sampled, and thus aliasing takes place along sub-sampled directions such as phase-encoding (PE) and slice-phase-encoding (SPE, for 3D imaging only) (Fig. \ref{fig:acceleratedMRI}b). In the image domain, a receptive field as small as one will be sufficient for de-aliasing along RO, since signals have no aliasing along this direction. While as for PE and SPE, the ideal receptive field for de-aliasing is as large as the FOV can cover. Therefore, it is desirable to utilize the global information along the sub-sampled directions for MRI reconstruction.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Figures/Fig1_TheSubSampling.png}
\caption{The demonstration of 3D k-space sampling with a uniform sub-sampling mask of rate 3 with a 10×10 fully sampled center. (a) The underlying image is sampled in k-space under the sub-Nyquist rate that caused aliasing in the image domain. Noticing the sub-sampling in k-space is essentially a point-wise multiplication with the underlying k-space and a binary mask, which is equivalent to circular convolution in the image domain, using a kernel as large as the entire FOV. (b) The binary sub-sampling mask and its equivalent image domain convolution kernel in 2D views. Due to RO is always fully sampled, the convolution kernel has only one non-zero line in the RO-PE and RO-SPE plane, indicating no aliasing along the RO direction. PE and SPE are both sub-sampled, leading to aliasing from both directions, which is equivalent to convolving the underlying image with a 2D kernel as large as FOV.}
\label{fig:acceleratedMRI}
\end{figure}

However, CNN has a limited receptive field due to highly localized convolutions using small kernels.
Using large convolution kernels<|CIT-MASK|> can enlarge the receptive field but also lead to massive computation and training difficulties<|CIT-MASK|>, which limit their applicability in practice.
Alternatively, deformable convolution<|CIT-MASK|> is designed to convolve a limited amount of voxels within a learnable, and possibly large receptive field. Nevertheless, it is unsuitable for de-aliasing MRI images, since it requires information from all voxels within the FOV.
Recently, attention-based methods<|CIT-MASK|> have demonstrated state-of-the-art performance in various vision tasks, including MRI artifact removal

